  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 223 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: An upper trough is pivoting across Quebec and northern \nNew England/NY early this morning. Meanwhile, surface high pressure \nis building eastward across the Great Lakes. The upper trough is \nmainly producing just some scattered cloud cover, though some light \nrain has been noted over southern portions of St Lawrence County. \nThe incoming airmass associated with the high is quite dry, so \nanticipate this will wane over the next few hours as ridging moves \noverhead. This fair weather will settle over our area for the \nremainder of the week. Each day will feature plentiful sunshine, \nwith highs gradually increasing as we head into the weekend. We'll \nstart out with highs in the 70s to around 80F today, and topping out \nin the 80s to near 90F by Saturday and Sunday. Thankfully, humidity \nwill remain at comfortable levels as dewpoints are expected to be in \nthe 40s and 50s, perhaps nudging into the low 60s by Sunday. The dry \nair and mostly clear skies will result in optimal radiational \ncooling tonight and Thursday night, and lows will be in the mid 40s \nto mid 50s both nights. Winds could be a little on the breezy side \nthis afternoon, but these should lessen by tonight as the pressure \ngradient slackens. A cold front starts to approach from \nOntario/Great Lakes later Sunday, and some guidance indicates that a \nfew showers/thunderstorms may make it into northern NY before \nsunset. However, general expectations are the bulk of any \nprecipitation will hold off until after dark.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Upper level closed low pressure will rotate across the \nHudson Bay into Quebec for much of next week, sending a series of \nshortwaves through northern New York and Vermont. The first is set \nto move through Sunday night, though high pressure and drier air \nlocked in across Nova Scotia looks to hinder its eastward \nprogression through the forecast area and limit measurable \nprecipitation. Chances of a shower or thunderstorm Sunday night are \naround 0 to 25%, highest across northern New York. Southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow will keep low temperatures seasonable to warm in \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s Sunday night, then allow temps to climb into \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s on Monday despite scattered showers and \nthunderstorms (20 to 60% chance precip, highest in northern New \nYork) as moisture finally lifts into the region. Lingering showers \nand clouds Monday night will keep temperatures seasonably warm in \nthe mid 50s to mid 60s again before a weak surface low crosses the \nGreat Lakes and another low pressure strengthens in the mid-Atlantic \non Tuesday, bringing a more widespread round of showers and \nthunderstorms to the region. Tuesday afternoon looks like the most \nlikely period for some showers and thunderstorms with 60-80% chance \nof measurable precipitation as some deterministic models are \ndepicting the shortwave as a closed low aloft, but this may change \nas we get closer. As waves pass through next week, they will take \nwith them small chunks of heat and moisture, gradually knocking our \ntemperatures and humidity levels down into midweek. By next \nWednesday, highs will be widespread 70s, and lows should fall into \nthe 50s and lower 60s, all slightly below seasonal normals.",
